/**
  @mainpage %OpenMS GUI documentation

  The graphical tools of %OpenMS and the library they are built on, OpenMS_GUI.
  They are developed in the repository
  <a href="https://github.com/OpenMS/OpenMS-TOPP-GUI">OpenMS-TOPP-GUI</a> and are built and
  installed on top of an %OpenMS installation of the same version (the %OpenMS library, the
  TOPP tools and the shared data come from there).

  All aspects of using %OpenMS (installation, workflows, user tutorials, including those for
  TOPPView) are described on the
  <a href="https://openms.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html">%OpenMS ReadTheDocs website</a>.
  The documentation of the %OpenMS library and of all other TOPP tools is the
  <a href="https://archive.openms.de/openms/Documentation/release/latest/html/index.html">%OpenMS API reference</a>
  (<a href="https://archive.openms.de/openms/Documentation/nightly/latest/html/index.html">nightly</a>).

  <H3>Graphical tools</H3>
  <UL>
    <LI> @subpage TOPP_TOPPView - visualization and analysis of MS data
    <LI> @subpage TOPP_TOPPAS - design and execution of TOPP workflows
    <LI> @subpage TOPP_INIFileEditor - editor for the parameter (INI) files of TOPP tools
  </UL>

  <H3>TOPP tools that need the GUI library</H3>
  <UL>
    <LI> @subpage TOPP_ExecutePipeline - runs TOPPAS workflows on the command line
    <LI> @subpage TOPP_ImageCreator - writes an LC-MS map as an image
  </UL>

  <H3>Tutorials</H3>
  <UL>
    <LI> @subpage TOPPAS_tutorial
    <LI> @subpage GUI_developer_notes
  </UL>

  <H3>The GUI library</H3>
  The classes of the GUI library are grouped in the module @ref Visual (see also the class
  list). The code examples in <code>doc/code_examples/Tutorial_GUI_*.cpp</code> show how to use
  some of the widgets in an application of your own.
*/

/**
  @page GUI_developer_notes Developing the GUI layer

  @section GUI_dev_build Building

  OpenMS-TOPP-GUI is built against an installed %OpenMS (or the %OpenMS SDK) of the same version
  and Qt 6:
  @code
  cmake -S OpenMS-TOPP-GUI -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="<OpenMS prefix>;<Qt prefix>"
  cmake --build build
  ctest --test-dir build
  @endcode
  The repository's README.md lists all options.

  @section GUI_dev_widgets Using the widgets in your own application

  Link the target <code>OpenMS::OpenMS_GUI</code> of the CMake package <code>OpenMSGUI</code>
  (or ask for <code>find_package(OpenMS COMPONENTS GUI)</code>, which finds it when the GUI layer
  is installed into the %OpenMS prefix). The examples:
  <UL>
    <LI> <code>Tutorial_GUI_ListEditor.cpp</code>: @ref OpenMS::ListEditor
    <LI> <code>Tutorial_GUI_ParamEditor.cpp</code>: @ref OpenMS::ParamEditor, editing an INI file
    <LI> <code>Tutorial_GUI_Plot1D.cpp</code>: @ref OpenMS::Plot1DWidget, showing a spectrum
  </UL>
  They are built by the target <code>Tutorials_build</code> (with <code>-DENABLE_TUTORIALS=ON</code>).

  @section GUI_dev_tests How to add a new GUI test for a class

  Suppose you want to create a GUI class for the class called <code>MyClass</code>.
  To add a new GUI test:
  <ol>
  <li>Create the <code>MyClass_test.cpp</code> in <code>src/tests/class_tests/openms_gui/source</code>.</li>
  <li>Add it to <code>src/tests/class_tests/openms_gui/CMakeLists.txt</code> in the GUI section.</li>
  <li>Have a look at existing GUI tests, as they use the QT TestLib framework and not the %OpenMS macros.</li>
  </ol>
  Without a display, run the tests with <code>QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen</code>.
*/
